    kind: work_item  # ed36
    title: "ci-debug can't fetch Woodpecker step logs (agent can't self-diagnose CI failures)"  # ed36
    status: todo_hard  # ed36
    priority: 2  # ed36
    parent:  # ed36
    tags:  # ed36
    - ci  # ed36
    - tooling  # ed36
    - governance  # ed36
    isbefore: []  # ed36
    duplicate_of: []  # ed36
    not_duplicate_of: []  # ed36
    pr_ref:  # ed36
    description: "Woodpecker CI logs are behind Cloudflare Access; scripts/ci-debug logs reports 'not retrievable via the API', and GitHub's commit-status API exposes only the single combined pass/fail context (ci/woodpecker/pr/woodpecker) with no per-step logs. When CI fails, the agent cannot read WHY without human aid -- this cost a very long debugging detour on PR #19, where a coverage-gate failure (99% vs 100%) was invisible via the combined status and reproduced GREEN locally on py3.12. Proposal: wire a Woodpecker API token (or a Cloudflare Access service token) into 'ci-debug logs' so step logs are pullable headlessly. NOTE: scripts/ci-debug is a GOVERNANCE file -- the code change needs human approval; this item documents the need and requests it."  # ed36
    fields: {}  # ed36
- op: discuss  # 155f
  at: '2026-07-30T20:03:14Z'  # 155f
  by: agent  # 155f
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 155f
  clock: 2  # 155f
  nonce: 155f  # 155f
  message: "IMPLEMENTED AND CI-GREEN in PR #94 — awaiting owner merge. Branch jgstern-agent/fix/wi-solob-woodpecker-logs, HEAD 0f999bd7bf. NOT merged: GitHub returns HTTP 405 on every merge attempt (auto-pr, then merge-pr 94) while CI reports success, which is branch protection enforcing the CODEOWNERS rule on the governance files this touches. The owner approved the change and self-merge verbally, but that does not bypass the protection — a review on the PR is required. This is the guard working as designed.\n\nTHE MEASURED FINDING, which is the substantive part and is true regardless of the merge: ALLOWLISTING THE CI HOST IS NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT. The owner approved adding ci.revolving-tactile.com.de to ALLOWED_WEBSITES.md, and an authenticated-less GET of the run URL still returns 403 — Cloudflare Access fronts the instance and rejects at the edge before Woodpecker is reached. So TWO independent credentials are needed, not one:\n  CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID / CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET — a Cloudflare Access SERVICE TOKEN, sent as CF-Access-Client-* headers. Gets past the edge.\n  WOODPECKER_TOKEN — a Woodpecker API token, sent as Bearer. Authenticates to the app itself.\nBoth must be created by the owner (I neither can nor may mint them); the code reads them from .env alongside FORGEJO_TOKEN/HG_GITHUB_TOKEN and never echoes them.\n\nWHAT THE PR CONTAINS: _github_fetch_job_log in scripts/lib/github-api.sh was a pure stub — resolve the run URL from the commit status, print 'not retrievable', return 1. It now parses the pipeline coordinates out of the SAME target_url it already printed (so no new configuration is introduced) and fetches the log when both credentials are present. When either is absent it degrades to EXACTLY the previous message plus a line naming what to set — strictly more capable, never less. Verified both ways: bash -n syntax check, and exercised against a real failed SHA with no credentials present (prints the URL + setup instructions, returns 1 as before).\n\nWHY THIS WAS WORTH DOING NOW: this session paid the cost directly. PR #92 failed CI twice and I spent two blind re-runs — a full CI cycle each — before the owner read the log and named the cause in one line (a stale docs/ARCHITECTURE.md tripping the 15-commit recency window, nothing to do with the change under review). That is the exact failure mode this item describes: 'agent can't self-diagnose CI failures'.\n\nTO FINISH: (1) owner reviews/merges PR #94; (2) owner creates a Cloudflare Access service token and a Woodpecker API token and puts the three vars in .env. After that 'ci-debug logs <job> <sha>' returns real logs and this closes."  # 155f
- op: discuss  # c4e2
  at: '2026-07-30T20:39:52Z'  # c4e2
  by: agent  # c4e2
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c4e2
  clock: 3  # c4e2
  nonce: c4e2  # c4e2
  message: "PR #94 MERGED by the owner (dev 01962c65c1). Verified on dev: the CF-Access header path is present in scripts/lib/github-api.sh and ci.revolving-tactile.com.de is in ALLOWED_WEBSITES.md.\n\nKEEPING THIS OPEN — deliberately, and this is a closure-evidence call rather than caution for its own sake. The item's statement is a RUNTIME claim ('ci-debug can't fetch Woodpecker step logs — agent can't self-diagnose CI failures'). Closing it on 'the code merged' would be a proxy closure of exactly the INV-nufob kind. Checked the three env vars on dev (existence only, values never read or printed): WOODPECKER_TOKEN, CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID and CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET are ALL ABSENT, so the runtime behaviour is unchanged — I re-ran 'ci-debug logs woodpecker 6435792229c5' against a real failed SHA on merged dev and it still cannot retrieve the log. The stated defect therefore still reproduces.\n\nWHAT DID CHANGE, and it is worth having: the failure is now ACTIONABLE instead of terminal. The old output said the logs are 'not retrievable via the API' full stop. It now names the three variables to set and states the finding that makes them non-obvious — that allowlisting the host is NOT sufficient, because an unauthenticated GET returns 403 at the Cloudflare Access edge before Woodpecker is reached. That distinction cost this session two blind CI re-runs to discover.\n\nTHE ONE REMAINING STEP IS THE OWNER'S, and it is not something I can do or should: create a Cloudflare Access service token and a Woodpecker API token, and put the three vars in .env. I neither can nor may mint credentials (AGENTS.md restricts me to FORGEJO_TOKEN / HG_GITHUB_TOKEN).\n\nCLOSE CONDITION, concrete and behavioral so the next session does not have to re-derive it: with the three vars set, './scripts/ci-debug logs woodpecker <a-known-failed-sha>' returns actual log text rather than the fallback block. Cite that output as the closure evidence. If it returns 'Woodpecker log fetch failed (HTTP nnn)', the credentials are present but wrong — a 403 means the CF service token is not authorized for the app, a 401 means the Woodpecker token is bad; the two are independent and the message distinguishes them."  # c4e2
