- op: discuss  # db6f
  at: '2026-07-30T21:29:28Z'  # db6f
  by: agent  # db6f
  actor: jgstern_agent  # db6f
  clock: 5  # db6f
  nonce: db6f  # db6f
  message: "DONE — dev 879dc180c6 (PRs #94, #97, #98). Closed on BEHAVIORAL evidence, not on 'the code merged'.\n\nTHE CLOSURE EVIDENCE. On merged dev, './scripts/ci-debug logs \"\" 6435792229c5' now returns the actual Woodpecker log for the failed step, ending in:\n    + ./scripts/generate-architecture --check\n    ERROR: docs/ARCHITECTURE.md is out of date: generated 17 commits ago, exceeds the 15-commit window\n    Run: ./scripts/generate-architecture\nThat is the real text of PR #92's failure — the exact thing the agent could not see, which is the defect this item names. Two further runs prove the selection actually discriminates rather than always returning the failure: 'logs verify-generated <sha>' gives the same ERROR, while 'logs pytest <sha>' gives a DIFFERENT step's log (TOTAL 183 0 100%).\n\nIT TOOK THREE FIXES, and only the first was the one originally filed.\n(1) Reachability — the host is behind Cloudflare Access. Allowlisting alone is NOT sufficient: an unauthenticated GET is 302-redirected to the Access login and never reaches Woodpecker. Two independent credentials are required — a Cloudflare Access SERVICE TOKEN (CF-Access-Client-Id/Secret, to pass the edge) and a Woodpecker API token (Bearer, for the app) — plus an Access policy whose ACTION is 'Service Auth' including that token. Creating the token is not enough on its own; that policy was the actual blocker on the owner's side.\n(2) Configuration — the server now comes from WOODPECKER_SERVER in .env rather than being written into a public repo. Only the pipeline coordinates are read off the commit status' target_url, and only its path, so a target_url cannot reintroduce a hard-coded host.\n(3) THE REAL BUG, which no amount of credentials would have fixed. The logs path had been inferred from the UI url, whose trailing number is the WORKFLOW INDEX, not the step. The endpoint keys on the step's GLOBAL id. Measured: /api/repos/1/logs/290/9 (pid) -> 404, /api/repos/1/logs/290/1653 (step id) -> 200, 116 KB. So a fetch is TWO calls — resolve the step from GET /api/repos/{repo}/pipelines/{number}, then GET /api/repos/{repo}/logs/{number}/{step_id} — and it now auto-selects the FAILED step when no job is named.\n\nDURABLE LESSON, and the reason this item was worth the effort: an inferred API shape is a premise, and it stayed invisible behind an auth wall. While the credentials were missing, EVERY failure looked like an auth failure, so the 404 that would have exposed the wrong path could not surface until Access opened. A blocked diagnostic hides the bugs in the diagnostic itself. Two decoding details were wrong for the same reason and were only corrected by reading a real response: entries can carry 'data: null' (naive joining raises TypeError) and each entry is ONE line with a 'line' ordinal, so they must be sorted and joined with newlines, not concatenated.\n\nCOST THIS ITEM WAS FILED TO PREVENT, now measured: PR #92 failed CI twice and two blind re-runs were spent — a full CI cycle each — before the owner read the log and named the cause in one line. That is exactly the 'agent can't self-diagnose CI failures' consequence, and it will not recur.\n\nRESIDUAL, disclosed: the fallback path (credentials absent) is exercised and correct, but the SUCCESS path has been verified only against this one Woodpecker instance and version. A different major version may move the endpoint again; the fallback now prints a 404 line naming that possibility so the next person recognises it immediately rather than suspecting auth."  # db6f
